# 0074-session-clock: the guest's clock comes from the session, not the PC (netplay determinism)
#
# What it fixes: host time leaks into the guest state in several places, so two players on different PCs (or two
# runs on one PC with a different TZ) see different times and desync. sceCdReadClock reads the PC's local time;
# the OSD time zone at boot is the PC's; the memory card's GetDir dates use the PC's clock and the card folder's
# file timestamps; fioGetstat reports the host disk's file times. With PS2Runtime::setSessionClock(epoch) set,
# every host time the guest can see becomes epoch + vsync_tick / 60 in UTC (time-zone offset 0 minutes):
# sceCdReadClock (in BCD, UTC), the OSD time zone (0), the memory card dates (the "." / ".." entries and the file
# times) and fioGetstat's file times. Unset, nothing changes. DON_SESSION_EPOCH=<seconds> sets the clock at runtime
# start, for the determinism checks without netplay.
# How it was verified: tests/runtime/session_clock_test.cpp (sceCdReadClock gives epoch in BCD UTC, the OSD offset
# is 0, GetDir dates equal the session time, and unset keeps the old behaviour); the multiplayer_match route with
# DON_SESSION_EPOCH=1700000000 and DON_TRACE_STATE=60 gives identical state lines with TZ=UTC and TZ=Asia/Tokyo.
# Original work (no other project).
#
diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h b/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h
index fcd03fc..ef29b49 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
 
